sections.body:
`map` applies a function to **every item** of an array and makes a **new array** from the results. The original array doesn't change:

```js
const prices = [10, 20, 30];
const doubled = prices.map((p) => p * 2);
console.log(doubled); // [20, 40, 60]
console.log(prices);  // [10, 20, 30]
```

The new array always has the same length as the old one. The function also receives a second value: the item's **position** (index, starting at 0).

```js
const names = ["Ada", "Ben"];
const lines = names.map((name, i) => (i + 1) + ". " + name);
console.log(lines); // ['1. Ada', '2. Ben']
```

examples.code:
const tanks = [80, 15, 60, 5, 45];

const percent = tanks.map((t) => t + "%");
const low = tanks.filter((t) => t < 20);
const total = tanks.reduce((sum, t) => sum + t, 0);

console.log("Tanks:", percent);
console.log("Running low:", low);
console.log("Total fuel:", total);
console.log("No empty tanks?", tanks.every((t) => t > 0));

const planets = [
  { name: "Mars", distance: 228 },
  { name: "Mercury", distance: 58 },
  { name: "Jupiter", distance: 778 },
  { name: "Venus", distance: 108 },
];

const order = planets
  .slice()
  .sort((a, b) => a.distance - b.distance)
  .map((p, i) => (i + 1) + ". " + p.name);

console.log(order.join("\n"));
console.log("First far planet:", planets.find((p) => p.distance > 500).name);

